    "left": "(P ⇒ Q) ∨ R",
    "right": "~((P ∧ ~Q) ∧ ~R)",
    "decide": true
  },
  "answer": "equivalent",
  "mistakes": [],
  "hints": [
    "Push the outer ∼ of ∼((P ∧ ∼Q) ∧ ∼R) inside with DeMorgan's law.",
    "∼(P ∧ ∼Q) is the same as P ⇒ Q: an implication fails only when P is true and Q is false."
  ],
  "solution": [
    "By DeMorgan's law, $\\sim ((P \\wedge \\sim Q) \\wedge \\sim R) = \\sim (P \\wedge \\sim Q) \\vee \\sim \\sim R = \\sim (P \\wedge \\sim Q) \\vee R$.",
    "Again by DeMorgan, $\\sim (P \\wedge \\sim Q) = \\sim P \\vee Q$, which is equivalent to $P \\Rightarrow Q$.",
    "So the right statement equals $(P \\Rightarrow Q) \\vee R$: they are logically equivalent. (Both columns are T T T F T T T T.)"
  ],
